--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
The darkness peels back like wet silk, and a single card drifts before you, suspended by nothing. It turns slowly, revealing The High Priestess—moon-pale face bisect between twin pillars of night and dawn. A hush settles over whatever space this is, as though the card itself inhales; you feel the hush travel through your ribs, a cool pressure behind your sternum that tastes of salt and secrecy.

A second card glides in from the left, lighter than paper should be. The Six of Cups: two children beside a stone basin in what must be the old Sun-Quarter of Aurelion, scarlet blossoms floating in the water. When the image locks upright, your mouth floods with the memory of honeyed milk, and for an instant the copper scent of the void is replaced by sun-warmed terracotta.

Lastly comes the Knight of Swords, galloping out of the dark at full tilt, cloak snapping like a banner over the ramparts of Tharros. The card stops an inch from your eyes; you feel wind-raw velocity whip across your skin, carrying flecks of volcanic grit that sting like cold sparks. Your pulse answers the drum of unseen hooves.

These cards are now yours. They hum in your memory.

The void shreds under that vibration, threads of night pulling apart like old canvas. Light—ordinary, dawn-colored light—spills through the tear, and you tumble forward into the waking world, boots striking damp flagstone that smells of moss and last night’s rain.

