The old man’s plea still hangs in the air when a new voice cuts across the square. A lanky merchant in a salt-stained linen coat hurries toward you, boots splashing through the puddles left by last night’s rain. He glances once at the elder, then fixes his reddened eyes on you. “Forgive the interruption, but you carry the look of someone who doesn’t scare easy,” he says, wringing his cap between his hands. “Name’s Callum. My cart was ransacked while I slept in the loft above the chandlery. They took nearly everything, yet left one thing behind—my sample lockbox, wedged under a root at the forest edge. I’d fetch it myself, but the path’s gloomier than a debtor’s heart after dusk, and I’ve no steel to speak of.”

He fishes a brass key from his pocket and presses it into your palm; the metal is still warm from his grip. “It’s nothing grand—just carved bone toggles and vials of whisper-oil for the Aurelion trade—but it’s my name on the lid, and without it I can’t prove my wares are honest. If you bring it back, the favor is worth more than coin to me.” Behind him, the old man with the letter retreats a step, watching, waiting, unwilling to leave until he knows whether decency still walks the realm.

