The wind that stirs the dust of Old Well Square smells of damp stone and yesterday’s bread; it lifts the hem of your coat and carries with it the creak of Callum’s empty cart. The merchant himself is a thin, flax-haired man in a leather apron, pacing a groove in the cobbles while he clutches the rim of the stone well as though it might offer advice. When he sees you hesitate, he wipes his palms on his thighs and speaks in a rush, voice low so the market gossip won’t catch it. “I’m no lord to offer coin I don’t have,” he says, eyes flicking toward the alley that leads east, “but my samples—little carved cedar boxes, no bigger than your two fists—were left at the forest edge when the raiders scattered. If you walk the fringe path at dawn, you’ll spot the lockbox beneath the first ash tree; the brass clasp catches the light like a candle stub.” His knuckles whiten on the well-coping. “I’d go myself, but my boy’s got fever and the wife’s crying for willow bark.”

You feel the grit of the square through your boot soles, ordinary as bread, yet the same breeze that lifts the ash leaves out there now tugs at your sleeve as if urging your feet toward the Whispering Forest Edge. Callum presses a folded scrap of parchment into your hand—directions inked in walnut dye, the paper still warm from his pocket—and manages a grateful nod that costs him visible effort. No magic spark dances at your fingertips, no sword rattles on your hip; only the earthy scent of cedar shavings clings to the note, promising that even a common man may carry something precious back through the morning mist.

