The chill of dawn still clings to the cobblestones of Old Well Square as you step beneath the leaning timber arcades that frame the marketplace. A single merchant in a patched wool coat paces a tight circle around the mossy lip of the well, boots scuffing grit across the stone. When he spots you, relief floods his wind-rened cheeks and he lifts a trembling hand. “Please—could you spare a moment? My cart was ransacked last night while I slept in the hostelry. They took the wheels, the canvas, even the horse’s feed bag, but what troubles me most is a little iron lockbox I left behind at the forest edge. Nothing grand, just trade samples—seeds, resin beads, a few vials of Aurelian perfume—yet without them I can’t prove my wares to buyers.” He glances toward the eastern gate where the road dissolves into the Whispering Forest, uncertainty flickering behind his eyes. “It’s only a short walk along the old mill path, and I’d fetch it myself, but my knees haven’t stopped shaking since I saw the splinters where my cart used to be.”

