The cracked flagstones of Old Well Square radiate the day’s heat up through your boot-soles. A short, sun-creased man in a faded linen vest keeps circling the mossy rim of the well, muttering numbers under his breath as if counting invisible losses. When his restless path brings him face-to-face with you, he startles, then exhales a shaky’s sigh. “Name’s Callum—herbs and dyes, nothing grand, but it’s mine.” His voice trembles like the quince-colored scarf knotted at his neck. “Last night someone levered my cart open, took thet coin pouch I’d hidden under the axle. They missed the lockbox, though—left it sitting like an insult at the forest edge, two bow-shots down the east path. I’d fetch it myself, but my knees turn watery soon as pines start whispering.” He glances toward the green-black wall of pines beyond the square’s archway, then back at you, eyes bright with a plea he’s too proud to voice outright. “Samples inside—indigo cakes, saffron threads—won’t feed a dragon, but they’ll keep my stall breathing another week. If you’re walking that way anyhow… I’d owe you the first pick of tomorrow’s dye bath, no charge.”

